# See https://help.github.com/articles/ignoring-files/ for more about ignoring files.

# dependencies
/node_modules
/.pnp
.pnp.*
.yarn/*
!.yarn/patches
!.yarn/plugins
!.yarn/releases
!.yarn/versions

# testing
/coverage

# next.js
/.next/
/out/

# production
/build

# misc
.DS_Store
*.pem

# debug
npm-debug.log*
yarn-debug.log*
yarn-error.log*
.pnpm-debug.log*

# env files (can opt-in for committing if needed)
.env*

# vercel
.vercel

# typescript
*.tsbuildinfo
next-env.d.ts

# Raw photo drop-ins at the public/ root.
# Every asset the site actually serves lives in a subfolder (brand, hero, partners,
# programs, about) and is committed. These are the untouched camera originals dropped
# here before processing: ~97MB against 6MB of derived assets, and they would also be
# served publicly at URLs containing spaces. Keep them locally, out of git history.
/public/*.jpg
/public/*.jpeg
/public/*.JPG
/public/*.JPEG
/public/*.png
/public/*.PNG

# Camera raw. Not a format any browser can display, so a raw file under public/ is never
# something the site serves, only something that got dropped in the same folder as the
# shots it was developed from. Three 25MB .CR2 files reached the repo this way and were
# publicly downloadable: the list above did not cover them because it only names the
# formats the site does use.
/public/*.CR2
/public/*.cr2
/public/*.NEF
/public/*.nef
/public/*.ARW
/public/*.arw
/public/*.dng
/public/*.DNG

# Loose media sitting at the repo root.
#
# Nothing the site serves lives here: assets go in public/, and the root holds config and
# nothing else. Files land here by being dropped into the project folder, and a `git add -A`
# will happily commit them. That is how five unrelated files, about 35MB of personal images
# and a video, reached a public repo in one commit.
/*.png
/*.PNG
/*.jpg
/*.JPG
/*.jpeg
/*.JPEG
/*.mp4
/*.MP4
/*.mov
/*.MOV
